    <!-- INTRODUCTION -->

    <section class="article-section reading-column opening">
      <p class="lead">
        William James’s relationship with homeopathy is more interesting
        than either a simple endorsement or a simple rejection.
      </p>

      <p>
        James earned his medical degree from Harvard in 1869, but his
        intellectual life soon moved far beyond conventional medical practice.
        He became one of the central figures in the development of American
        psychology and philosophy, publishing
        <em> The Principles of Psychology </em>
        in 1890 and
        <em> The Varieties of Religious Experience </em>
        in 1902.
      </p>

      <p>
        His thinking was marked by a persistent interest in experience:
        what people actually observed, what happened when an idea was tested,
        and whether accepted theories adequately accounted for the facts.
      </p>

      <p>
        Homeopathy entered that story remarkably early—and it continued
        to appear throughout much of his life.
      </p>

      <p>
        Rather than beginning with later claims about what James supposedly
        believed, I followed the documentary record backward:{" "}
        <strong>
          James’s medical notebooks, family correspondence, published
          letters, public writings, and surviving manuscripts.
        </strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        Read chronologically, they reveal a relationship with homeopathy
        extending from his years as a young physician into the final years
        of his life.
      </p>

      <aside class="evidence-note archival-research" aria-labelledby="archival-note-title">
        <p class="source-label">About My Research</p>

        <h3 id="archival-note-title">
          A Note on the Archival Research
        </h3>

        <p>
          In researching this article, I traced references in the scholarly
          literature back to their underlying archival documents at Harvard
          University’s Houghton Library. I identified and requested copies
          of manuscripts including James’s medical-era Notebook 26,
          Mary Walsh James’s September 21, 1869 letter, and correspondence
          connected with James’s 1909 treatment by Dr. James Ralph Taylor Jr.
        </p>

        <p>
          At the time of this writing, I had not obtained copies of those
          manuscripts. Where I could not examine an original myself,
          I relied on published correspondence and scholarship that identifies
          and discusses the underlying documents.
        </p>

        <p>
          One important exception is William James’s February 27, 1903 letter
          to Henry William Rankin. Houghton Library provided me with a scan
          of that original manuscript, which I examined myself and reproduce
          in this article.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            Throughout the article, my source notes distinguish between
            manuscripts I examined myself, archival documents I know through
            scholarship, and correspondence available in published editions.
          </strong>
        </p>

        <p class="research-directions">
          Each historical section ends with a compact source note and links
          for further examination. External source links open in a new tab.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- MEDICAL SCHOOL NOTEBOOK -->

    <section class="feature-band" id="notebook-26">
      <div class="reading-column">
        <p class="section-label">c. 1868–1869</p>

        <h2>
          A Medical Notebook Entry:<br />
          “Similia Similibus Curantur”
        </h2>

        <p>
          The earliest evidence I have found comes from James’s own
          medical-era notebooks.
        </p>

        <p>
          Among the surviving William James papers at Harvard is
          <strong> Notebook 26</strong>, a collection of notes begun in the
          1860s and continued over a period of years.
        </p>

        <p>
          Scholarship examining James’s reading notes identifies an entry
          filed under the Latin phrase
          <strong><em> “Similia similibus curantur”</em></strong>—
          “like cures like,” the principle at the center of homeopathy.
        </p>

        <p>
          This should not be read as proof that the young James had already
          embraced homeopathy. What it does show is that the principle was
          among the therapeutic questions he considered seriously enough
          to enter into his medical notes.
        </p>

        <aside class="section-source" aria-labelledby="notebook-source-title">
          <h3 class="source-label" id="notebook-source-title">
            Source &amp; Evidence
          </h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Archival manuscript identified through scholarship.</strong>
            William James, Notebook 26, entry headed
            <em> “Similia similibus curantur,” </em>
            c. 1868–1869. Houghton Library, Harvard University.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paul J. Croce identifies the entry in “James and Medicine,”
            in <em>The Oxford Handbook of William James</em>.
            His bibliography describes the notebook as spanning
            c. 1864–1874 and the entry as arranged alphabetically by title.
          </p>

          <p class="source-note">
            I have not examined this notebook myself. My account follows
            Croce’s scholarly identification of the manuscript.
          </p>

