        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WILKINSON, 1869 -->

    <section class="article-section reading-column" id="wilkinson-1869">
      <p class="section-label">September 1869</p>

      <h2>James Becomes the Patient</h2>

      <p>
        The documentary trail becomes more personal almost immediately
        after James completed his medical degree.
      </p>

      <p>
        James John Garth Wilkinson—a physician, family friend, and prominent
        nineteenth-century homeopath—became involved in William’s care.
      </p>

      <p>
        The underlying evidence comes from William’s own family
        correspondence, which I have followed through published scholarship.
      </p>

      <p>
        On September 21, 1869, William’s mother,{" "}
        <strong> Mary Walsh James</strong>, wrote to his brother Henry.
        Scholarship citing this manuscript identifies Wilkinson’s
        prescription as{" "}
        <strong> high dilutions of Rhus and Nux vomica</strong>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Henry subsequently wrote to William that he was
        <strong> “extremely interested” </strong>
        in what their mother had reported about Wilkinson’s
        <strong> “diagnosis &amp; prescription.”</strong>
      </p>

      <aside class="section-source" aria-labelledby="wilkinson-source-title">
        <h3 class="source-label" id="wilkinson-source-title">
          Source &amp; Evidence
        </h3>

        <p>
          <strong>Family correspondence accessed through scholarship.</strong>
          {" "}Mary Walsh James to Henry James Jr., September 21, 1869,
          Houghton Library; and Henry’s subsequent letter to William,
          cited in <em>The Correspondence of William James</em>,
          vol. 1, p. 106.
        </p>

        <p>
          Croce connects these documents in
          <em> Young William James Thinking</em>, p. 112 and accompanying
          notes, and in “James and Medicine.”
        </p>

        <p class="source-note">
          I have not examined Mary’s original letter myself. I follow
          Croce’s account of the prescription and his citations to the
          family correspondence.
        </p>

