    <!-- HYDRASTIS, 1872 -->

    <section class="feature-band soft" id="hydrastis-1872">
      <div class="reading-column">
        <p class="section-label">November 24, 1872</p>

        <h2>“A Homeopathic Remedy”</h2>

        <p>
          A few years later, James’s own correspondence provides another
          intriguing piece of the record.
        </p>

        <p>
          On November 24, 1872, James wrote to his brother Henry that he
          had found a homeopathic preparation of
          <strong> Hydrastis </strong>
          beneficial for constipation.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="pull-quote">
          <p>
            “I have found a homeopathic remedy, hydrastis … to be of
            decided efficacy for constip[atio]n.”
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="source-line">
          — William James to Henry James Jr., November 24, 1872,
          <em> The Correspondence of William James</em>,
          vol. 1, p. 178; quoted in Paul J. Croce,
          <em> Young William James Thinking</em>.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is significant because James was no longer merely reading
          about a therapeutic principle or hearing about a prescription
          chosen for him.
        </p>

        <p>
          He was describing a remedy he himself identified as homeopathic
          and reporting the result he believed he had experienced.
        </p>

        <aside class="section-source" aria-labelledby="hydrastis-source-title">
          <h3 class="source-label" id="hydrastis-source-title">
            Source &amp; Evidence
          </h3>

          <p>
