    <!-- MEDICAL REGISTRATION ADDRESS, 1898 -->

    <section class="article-section reading-column" id="medical-registration-1898">
      <p class="section-label">February 1898</p>

      <h2>Homeopathy Enters James’s Public Argument</h2>

      <p>
        Nearly three decades after Wilkinson treated him, James confronted
        homeopathy from a very different position.
      </p>

      <p>
        In 1898, Massachusetts was debating a medical-registration bill that would have restricted 
        who could legally offer treatment. James objected to provisions that would require practitioners 
        of other healing approaches to meet the medical profession’s prescribed qualifications, 
        effectively excluding their work before its claims could be fairly investigated. 
        His concern was that legal authority would settle questions he believed should remain open to observation and inquiry.
      </p>

      <p>
        In his <em>Address on the Medical Registration Bill</em>,
        James specifically raised homeopathy.
      </p>

      <blockquote class="quote-card">
        <p>
          “An enormous mass of experience, both of homeopathic doctors and
          their patients, is invoked in favor of the efficacy of these
          remedies and doses.”
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        The importance of the statement lies in what James was—and was
        not—arguing.
      </p>

      <p>
        He was not declaring that every claim made for homeopathy had
        therefore been proved.
      </p>

      <p>
        He was challenging the assumption that such a body of reported
        experience could simply be dismissed without investigation.
      </p>

      <p>
        For James, the question was methodological:{" "}
        <strong>
          should medicine close inquiry by definition, or allow experience
          to test the theory?
        </strong>
      </p>

      <aside class="section-source" aria-labelledby="address-source-title">
        <h3 class="source-label" id="address-source-title">
          Source &amp; Evidence
        </h3>

        <p>
          <strong>Published primary source. </strong>
          William James, “Address on the Medical Registration Bill”
          (1898), reprinted in
          <em> Essays, Comments, and Reviews</em>,
          Harvard University Press, 1987, pp. 56–62.
        </p>

        <p>
          A later historical reproduction in the
          <em> Congressional Record </em>
          includes James’s discussion of the reported experience of
          homeopathic physicians and patients.
        </p>

        <p class="source-note">
          The government-hosted PDF provides a historical reproduction of
          James’s argument. Its inclusion in the Congressional Record
          is not a government endorsement of the medical claims discussed.
        </p>

