    <!-- PUTNAM LETTER -->

    <section class="primary-source" id="putnam-1898">
      <div class="reading-column">
        <p class="section-label">March 2, 1898</p>

        <h2>“Why Seek to Stop” the Experiences?</h2>

        <p>
          In a letter dated March 2, 1898, James wrote privately to
          neurologist <strong>James Jackson Putnam</strong> about his
          protest against the medical-license bill.
        </p>

        <p>
          The private letter helps explain the principle behind his
          public argument.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="major-quote">
          <p>
            “Why seek to stop the really extremely important experiences
            which these peculiar creatures are rolling up?”
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          The wording is characteristically Jamesian.
          He could be skeptical, humorous, even irreverent toward people
          whose claims did not fit established categories.
        </p>

        <p>
          But ridicule was not, for him, a substitute for investigation.
        </p>

        <p>
          The accumulated experiences themselves remained evidence that
          deserved examination.
        </p>

        <aside class="section-source" aria-labelledby="putnam-source-title">
          <h3 class="source-label" id="putnam-source-title">
            Source &amp; Evidence
          </h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Published primary correspondence.{" "}</strong>
            William James to James Jackson Putnam, March 2, 1898.
            <em> The Letters of William James</em>,
            edited by his son Henry James, vol. II,
            Atlantic Monthly Press, 1920, pp. 66–67.
          </p>

          <p>
            The complete letter is available through Project Gutenberg.
            It places the quoted question within James’s opposition to
            the proposed legislation and his concern about the reaction
            of his medical colleagues.
          </p>

          <p class="source-note">
            This letter discusses unconventional healing more broadly;
            the quoted sentence is not an isolated statement about
            homeopathy alone.
          </p>

