        <div class="reading-column">
          <p class="section-label">February 27, 1903</p>

          <h2>The Manuscript Changes the Story</h2>

          <p>
            Five years after the medical-registration debate,
            James addressed homeopathy again—this time in a private
            handwritten letter.
          </p>

          <p>
            On February 27, 1903, James wrote to
            <strong> Henry William Rankin</strong>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The original manuscript survives in the Houghton Library at
            Harvard University.
          </p>

          <blockquote class="pull-quote">
            <p>
              “I always believe that homeopathy should get a fair trial in
              obstinate chronic cases.”
            </p>
          </blockquote>

          <blockquote class="pull-quote second">
            <p>
              “I know that homeopathic remedies are not inert, as orthodox
              medicine insists that they necessarily must be.”
            </p>
          </blockquote>

          <p>The distinction matters.</p>

          <p>
            James was not merely defending someone else’s right to experiment.
            He now made a direct statement about the remedies themselves.
          </p>

          <p>
            The letter does not establish that he endorsed every doctrine
            associated with homeopathy. It does show that he rejected the
            assertion that the remedies must necessarily be inert.
          </p>

          <aside class="evidence-note" aria-labelledby="handwriting-title">
            <h3 id="handwriting-title">
              Reading the Original Handwriting
            </h3>

            <p>
              Published transcriptions of this letter have sometimes rendered
              James’s first sentence as
              <strong> “I always believed…”</strong>
            </p>

            <p>
              When I examined the scan supplied by Houghton Library,
              I read the wording as
              <strong> “I always believe…”</strong>
            </p>

            <p>
              That is my reading of the handwriting, rather than a claim
              that every published transcription has been definitively
              corrected. I reproduce the manuscript so readers can examine
              the wording for themselves.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <aside class="section-source" aria-labelledby="rankin-source-title">
            <h3 class="source-label" id="rankin-source-title">
              Source &amp; Evidence
            </h3>

            <p>
              <strong>Original manuscript examined. </strong>
              William James to Henry William Rankin,
              February 27, 1903.
              Houghton Library, Harvard University,
              <strong> MS Am 2355 (30)</strong>.
            </p>

            <p>
              Houghton Library supplied the manuscript scan reproduced here.
              I examined that scan myself when preparing this article.
            </p>

          </aside>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TAYLOR, 1909 -->

    <section class="feature-band" id="taylor-1909">
      <div class="reading-column">
        <p class="section-label">1909</p>

        <h2>James Returns to Homeopathic Care</h2>

        <p>
          The documentary trail does not end with the Rankin letter.
        </p>

        <p>
          By 1909, James’s health had become increasingly difficult.
          During this period he came under the care of
          <strong> Dr. James Ralph Taylor Jr.</strong>,
          a physician identified in scholarship on James as a homeopathic
          practitioner.
        </p>

        <p>
          James’s letters to his brother Henry describe Taylor in initially
          skeptical terms, but they also document a sustained course of
          treatment.
        </p>

        <p>
          The regimen was not exclusively homeopathic.
          James described a mixture of approaches that included homeopathic
          medicines along with several physical and electrical treatments.
        </p>

        <p>
          The surviving correspondence is therefore particularly valuable:
          it allows Taylor’s homeopathic treatment to be documented without
          pretending that it was the only therapy James received.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="quote-card">
          <p>
            James referred specifically to Taylor’s
            “homoeopathic pellets.”
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          What seems to have impressed James increasingly, however, was not
          simply a medicine. It was Taylor’s interpretation of the way
          James lived.
        </p>

        <p>
          James wrote admiringly of Taylor’s
          <strong> “extraordinarily shrewd perceptions” </strong>
          concerning what he called
          <strong> “the dynamics of the human machine.”</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          Taylor believed James habitually lived at too intense a pitch:
          racing mentally, anticipating events, bracing himself against
          circumstances, and carrying unnecessary tension into both thought
          and movement.
        </p>

        <p>
          James eventually summarized Taylor’s purpose in striking language:
          the real task, he wrote, was{" "}
          <strong>
            “to re-educate me as to my general way of holding myself in the
            current of life.”
          </strong>
        </p>

        <aside class="section-source" aria-labelledby="taylor-source-title">
          <h3 class="source-label" id="taylor-source-title">
            Source &amp; Evidence
          </h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Primary correspondence accessed through scholarship. </strong>
            James’s 1909 letters to his brother Henry,
            <em> The Correspondence of William James</em>,
            vol. 3: <em>William and Henry, 1897–1910</em>,
            edited by Ignas K. Skrupskelis and Elizabeth M. Berkeley,
            University Press of Virginia, 1994,
            pp. 376 and 386–387.
          </p>

          <p>
            Richard Shusterman discusses Taylor’s mixed regimen and James’s
            account of bodily tension and therapeutic re-education in
            “William James, Somatic Introspection, and Care of the Self.”
            Croce also discusses Taylor in “James and Medicine.”
          </p>

          <p class="source-note">
            I have not examined the original 1909 manuscripts myself.
            My account follows scholarship quoting and citing the published
            correspondence. The publisher link describes the edition;
            it does not provide the complete letters.
          </p>

