    <!-- TIMELINE -->

    <section class="article-section reading-column">
      <p class="section-label">Following the Record</p>

      <h2>More Than Forty Years of Evidence</h2>

      <p>
        No single document tells the whole story.
        The significance appears when the surviving evidence is placed
        chronologically.
      </p>

      <div class="timeline-card">
        <div class="timeline-item">
          <a class="timeline-year" href="#notebook-26">c. 1868–69</a>
          <p>
            Scholarship identifies an entry in James’s medical-era notebook
            filed under
            <strong><em> “Similia similibus curantur.”</em></strong>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <a class="timeline-year" href="#wilkinson-1869">1869</a>
          <p>
            Family correspondence records homeopathic physician
            <strong> James John Garth Wilkinson </strong>
            treating William and prescribing high dilutions of{" "}
            <strong> Rhus and Nux vomica</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <a class="timeline-year" href="#hydrastis-1872">1872</a>
          <p>
            James reports finding a
            <strong> “homeopathic remedy, hydrastis” </strong>
            of decided efficacy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <a class="timeline-year" href="#medical-registration-1898">1898</a>
          <p>
            James publicly invokes the accumulated experience of homeopathic
            physicians and patients while opposing restrictive medical
            registration. His letter to Putnam explains his broader concern
            about suppressing unconventional healing experience.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <a class="timeline-year" href="#rankin-1903">1903</a>
          <p>
            James writes that homeopathy should receive
            <strong> “a fair trial” </strong>
            and states that he knows homeopathic remedies are
            <strong> “not inert.”</strong>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <a class="timeline-year" href="#taylor-1909">1909</a>
          <p>
            James undergoes sustained treatment with
            <strong> Dr. James Ralph Taylor Jr.</strong>,
            including medicines he himself describes as
            <strong> “homoeopathic pellets.”</strong>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="timeline-note">
        Each date links back to the corresponding discussion and source note.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- CAREFUL READING -->

    <section class="primary-source record-section">
      <div class="reading-column">
        <p class="section-label">Reading the Sources Carefully</p>

        <h2>What Does the Evidence Actually Show?</h2>

        <p>
          It would be easy to turn this documentary record into a claim
          that William James
          <strong> “was a homeopath.”</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          I do not think the sources justify that simplification.
        </p>

        <p>
          James retained his independence of thought, and his comments
          about homeopathy were often qualified by skepticism.
          His 1909 treatment was also therapeutically mixed rather than
          exclusively homeopathic.
        </p>

        <p>
          But the opposite simplification is equally difficult to sustain.
        </p>

        <p>
          Homeopathy was not merely an abstract controversy James encountered
          once in a debate about medical freedom.
        </p>

        <p>
          The documentary record places it repeatedly within his life:
          during his medical education, within his family’s medical care,
          in remedies he personally used, in his public argument about medical
          evidence, in his private correspondence, and again in treatment
          late in life.
        </p>

        <p>
          I read these documents as evidence of James’s experiences and
          judgments in their historical setting. They do not, by themselves,
          establish the clinical effectiveness of homeopathy.
        </p>

        <p class="record-closing">
          My reading of the record leads to a narrower and more interesting
          historical conclusion:
          <strong>
            {" "}William James remained willing, across decades, to examine
            homeopathy by experience rather than dismiss it by definition.
          </strong>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHY IT MATTERS -->

    <section class="feature-band lasting-philosophy">
      <div class="lasting-inner">
        <p class="section-label">
          Why William James’s Story Matters
        </p>

        <h2>Experience Before Assumption</h2>

        <div class="lasting-copy">
          <p>
            James spent much of his intellectual life resisting systems that
            became so certain of their theories that they stopped listening
            to experience.
          </p>

          <p>
            That habit of mind appears throughout his psychology and
            philosophy, but the medical record gives it an unusually concrete
            form.
          </p>

          <p>
            Homeopathy confronted nineteenth-century medicine with claims
            that many physicians considered impossible on theoretical grounds.
          </p>

          <p>
            James’s response was characteristically his own:
            the theory did not get to decide in advance what experience
            was allowed to show.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="lasting-divider"></div>

        <div class="lasting-closing">
          <p class="closing-line">
            Read the reports.<br />
            Examine the experience.<br />
            Test the claim.<br />
            Then decide what the evidence supports.
          </p>

          <p>
            By 1903, James could state his position in a private letter
            with unusual clarity.
          </p>

          <blockquote class="closing-quote">
            <p>
              “I know that homeopathic remedies are not inert…”
            </p>
          </blockquote>

          <p class="final-lines">
            Not a quotation attributed to him generations later.<br />
            Not an argument reconstructed from reputation.<br />
            <strong>
              His words—preserved in his correspondence and now readable
              in the surviving manuscript itself.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FURTHER READING -->

    <section class="sources" aria-labelledby="further-reading-title">
      <div class="sources-inner">
        <p class="section-label">William James</p>

        <h2 id="further-reading-title">Further Reading</h2>

        <p class="further-intro">
          These works provide additional context for James’s psychology
          and philosophy. <br>The documentary sources for this article appear
